SUBJECT MATTER OF LOBBYING:
Advanced Education; Agriculture; Arts and Culture; COVID-19; Colleges & Universities; Economic Development and Trade; Finances and Budgets; Freedom of Information; Government Procurement; Health; Housing; Indigenous Relations and Reconciliation; Industry; Justice and Law Enforcement; Labour/Labour Relations; Mental Health and Addictions; Municipal Affairs; Non-Profit Organization; Poverty; Small Business; Sports and Recreation; Taxation and Finance; Tourism; Transportation (Virtual event with Minister Robinson speaking about BC Budget 2022. The minister was asked questions about government programs and plans for growing the economy, making changes to the payroll tax and consulting with the business community on issues such as paid sick days and investment in innovations. | The Greater Victoria Chamber of Commerce supports the Victoria Arts Council and its annual LOOK community art showcase and asked the minister to consider the Victoria Arts Council’s grant application in order to show support for local artists in our region. | The Greater Victoria Chamber of Commerce sent a letter supporting the proposal put forward by the Líl̓wat (Lil’wat), xʷməθkʷəy̓əm (Musqueam), Sḵwx̱wú7mesh (Squamish) and səlilwətaɬ (Tsleil-Waututh) to lead an Indigenous bid to host the 2030 Olympic and Paralympic Winter Games in British Columbia. | The Greater Victoria Chamber of Commerce co-signed a request asking:
1.	The provincial Police Services Division ensures Victoria adequately funds the Victoria Police Department;
2.	The Attorney General works with Prosecution Services to prevent repeat and habitual offenders from being released back into the community while awaiting trial;
3.	That the Province expedites the building of complex care facilities to house and treat the most marginalized, to establish their safety and to enhance community well-being.
4.	The Province works with the District of Saanich and City of Victoria to ensure the prompt continuation of the Citizens’ Assembly examining the benefits and challenges of merging the municipalities including their respective police departments. | The Greater Victoria Chamber and our 1,400 members would appreciate Minister Bailey's consideration to be included in ongoing conversations to offer our perspective on the Ministry’s priorities and policies, specifically the government's rebate programs to help businesses deal with costs related to vandalism. | The acceleration of investment in non-market housing that meets the needs of people who require homes but are not able to participate in the private real estate market. | Support for the continued redevelopment of Phase 3 of Belleville Terminal as a key international gateway between B.C. and external market and allow for improved customs and border services. | Support for directional and informational signage on Highway 17 on behalf of a member organization of the Greater Victoria Chamber of Commerce | Request to talk about ideas and innovations regarding affordable, temporary housing for people at risk. | Request for Minister Kahlon to speak to Greater Victoria business community at a chamber luncheon. | Request for meeting with Minister Popham to discuss ongoing concerns for business and safe community in Downtown Victoria. | Request for meeting and help arranging meeting with Minister of Housing to discuss challenges and opportunities for adding housing supply to Greater Victoria through the Bayview development. | Request for letter of support for the nomination of Gordon Planes to Senate. | Request for consultation with business groups to facilitate implementation of vaccine passport | Reminder about request to improve the Lobbyist Transparency Act, and request to discuss with ministry staff why doing so will benefit British Columbia communities. | Phone call that touched on funding for Indigenous Economic Reconciliation that The Chamber had asked about previously, as well as general business concerns. | Ongoing conversation about request for funding for The Greater Victoria Chamber of Commerce's Indigenous Economic Reconciliation Initiative. | Met with Minister of Land, Water and Resource Stewardship and Minister Responsible for Fisheries Josie Osborne at the Vancouver Island Economic Alliance Summit and discussed economic issues affecting Vancouver Island. | Letter urging Deputy Minister to the Premier on behalf of Greater Victoria employers and business community to fully consider the potentially catastrophic domino effect that changing the nature of public sector work could have on the economy of our provincial capital, relating to the proposal by the BC Public Service to disrupt its hiring practices and reduce the number of workers in downtown Victoria and in our region as a whole. | Letter in support of the Victoria Dragon Boat Festival Society’s application for the Tourism Events Program (TEP) to host the annual Dragon Boat Festival. | Invitation to Premier Eby to speak to Greater Victoria business community at a chamber luncheon. | In light of the pronounced role addiction and mental health issues plays in homeless populations, and the ongoing Opioid crisis, the Greater Victoria Chamber of Commerce actively supports the creation and enhancement of supports for mental health services and addiction recovery treatment. | Email introduction between BC government and FRS Clipper Ferry CEO to create connections and economic development opportunities between BC/Canada and Germany. | Discussion about ongoing work on Indigenous economic reconciliation. | Discussion about innovative housing solution based on model in use in Atlantic Canada. | Discussion about Indigenous businesses in Greater Victoria and opportunities to work together to connect them with regional business community. | Discussion about economic and community development potential of using office space leased by the provincial government that is not being fully used. | Conversation about housing proposed by Bayview in Victoria to discuss challenges and opportunities with the process. | British Columbia’s Lobbyists Transparency Act (2020) (LTA) has placed a considerable burden on the not-for-profit sector and smaller organizations, including Chambers of Commerce. This legislation adds administrative constraints to our ability to perform our traditional roles in our communities. We request the definition of "small organization" be amended to 25 employees or less and the act provide a full exemption for all small organizations and only require registration of actual acts of lobbying upon completion, increasing the 50-hour threshold to 200 hours for charities and non-profits and eliminate the requirement for Chambers of Commerce altogether. In addition, establish a more streamlined lobbying tracking report form and process and remove accumulated lobbying reporting requirements when working with other organizations. | BC recognize the need for responsible public health orders and guidelines that prioritizes the health of the public through a regional approach to further re-opening or restrictions  taking into consideration the fact that we are a massive province with significant variations in population density and rate of transmission of COVID-19. | Assistance in restoring bus service on Vancouver Island that is a vital connection for residents of Greater Victoria and Vancouver Island especially those who reside in Indigenous communities. | As BC enters Phase 3, we will meet with provincial officials including the Finance Minister and Tourism Minister to ask for details about BC's $1.5 billion fund to restart the economy. Working with other community organizations in Greater Victoria, The Chamber would like to see specific funding made available for tourism organizations directly affected by the pandemic. | As an additional element to our Indigenous engagement initiatives, we hope to add a multimedia component to a project celebrating the Chamber’s 160th anniversary. The Chamber was founded on Feb. 9, 1863, and we marked the date with a special gathering at the Royal BC Museum. At that time, we unveiled a website dedicated to the history of business and commerce on Vancouver Island through the perspectives of the diverse communities that call our region home. The project abides by the protocol of “nothing about us without us.” This is especially key to information about Indigenous communities, which requires working extensively with Indigenous knowledge keepers, including offering honorariums for elders and scholars to gain their perspective on traditions of commerce in our region. We are asking for help funding these proposed economic reconciliation initiatives. | A request for ensuring non-profits have access to government relief programs, specifically the BC Small and Medium Sized Business Recovery Grant. Non-profit organizations make significant contributions to our economy and have faced challenges similar to all business during the pandemic. | A phone call suggesting temporary repurposing of office space in Greater Victoria that is leased to the Provincial government but is vacant due to the Public Sector's shift to remote work. Rather than sit empty, the space could be used by non-profits and small businesses to add vibrancy to the area. | A letter supporting Northwest Deuce Days application to the Fairs, Festivals and Events Recovery Fund. | A letter requesting the ministry consider the Greater Victoria Dragon Boat Festival Society for funding under the Fairs, Festivals and Events Recovery Fund. | Advocacy around ongoing economic recovery and support for businesses, specifically sectors hardest hit by the pandemic. Minister was guest speaker at Chamber AGM.)

