Victoria Chamber of Commerce's Indigenous Economic Reconciliation Initiative. | Met with Minister of Land, Water and Resource Stewardship and Minister Responsible for Fisheries Josie Osborne at the Vancouver Island Economic Alliance Summit and discussed economic issues affecting Vancouver Island. | Letter urging Deputy Minister to the Premier on behalf of Greater Victoria employers and business community to fully consider the potentially catastrophic domino effect that changing the nature of public sector work could have on the economy of our provincial capital, relating to the proposal by the BC Public Service to disrupt its hiring practices and reduce the number of workers in downtown Victoria and in our region as a whole. | Letter in support of the Victoria Dragon Boat Festival Society’s application for the Tourism Events Program (TEP) to host the annual Dragon Boat Festival. | Invitation to Premier Eby to speak to Greater Victoria business community at a chamber luncheon. | In light of the pronounced role addiction and mental health issues plays in homeless populations, and the ongoing Opioid crisis, the Greater Victoria Chamber of Commerce actively supports the creation and enhancement of supports for mental health services and addiction recovery treatment. | Discussion about ongoing work on Indigenous economic reconciliation. | Discussion about innovative housing solution based on model in use in Atlantic Canada. | Discussion about Indigenous businesses in Greater Victoria and opportunities to work together to connect them with regional business community. | Discussion about economic and community development potential of using office space leased by the provincial government that is not being fully used. | Conversation about housing proposed by Bayview in Victoria to discuss challenges and opportunities with the process. | British Columbia’s Lobbyists Transparency Act (2020) (LTA) has placed a considerable burden on the not-for-profit sector and smaller organizations, including Chambers of Commerce. This legislation adds administrative constraints to our ability to perform our traditional roles in our communities. We request the definition of "small organization" be amended to 25 employees or less and the act provide a full exemption for all small organizations and only require registration of actual acts of lobbying upon completion, increasing the 50-hour threshold to 200 hours for charities and non-profits and eliminate the requirement for Chambers of Commerce altogether. In addition, establish a more streamlined lobbying tracking report form and process and remove accumulated lobbying reporting requirements when working with other organizations. | BC recognize the need for responsible public health orders and guidelines that prioritizes the health of the public through a regional approach to further re-opening or restrictions  taking into consideration the fact that we are a massive province with significant variations in population density and rate of transmission of COVID-19. | Assistance in restoring bus service on Vancouver Island that is a vital connection for residents of Greater Victoria and Vancouver Island especially those who reside in Indigenous communities. | As BC enters Phase 3, we will meet with provincial officials including the Finance Minister and Tourism Minister to ask for details about BC's $1.5 billion fund to restart the economy. Working with other community organizations in Greater Victoria, The Chamber would like to see specific funding made available for tourism organizations directly affected by the pandemic. | As an additional element to our Indigenous engagement initiatives, we hope to add a multimedia component to a project celebrating the Chamber’s 160th anniversary. The Chamber was founded on Feb. 9, 1863, and we marked the date with a special gathering at the Royal BC Museum. At that time, we unveiled a website dedicated to the history of business and commerce on Vancouver Island through the perspectives of the diverse communities that call our region home. The project abides by the protocol of “nothing about us without us.” This is especially key to information about Indigenous communities, which requires working extensively with Indigenous knowledge keepers, including offering honorariums for elders and scholars to gain their perspective on traditions of commerce in our region. We are asking for help funding these proposed economic reconciliation initiatives. | A request for ensuring non-profits have access to government relief programs, specifically the BC Small and Medium Sized Business Recovery Grant. Non-profit organizations make significant contributions to our economy and have faced challenges similar to all business during the pandemic. | A phone call suggesting temporary repurposing of office space in Greater Victoria that is leased to the Provincial government but is vacant due to the Public Sector's shift to remote work. Rather than sit empty, the space could be used by non-profits and small businesses to add vibrancy to the area. | A letter supporting Northwest Deuce Days application to the Fairs, Festivals and Events Recovery Fund. | A letter requesting the ministry consider the Greater Victoria Dragon Boat Festival Society for funding under the Fairs, Festivals and Events Recovery Fund. | Advocacy around ongoing economic recovery and support for businesses, specifically sectors hardest hit by the pandemic. Minister was guest speaker at Chamber AGM.) BC Ministries/Provincial Entities: Children and Family Development, Finance, Labour, Office of the Premier, Public Safety and Solicitor General, Camosun College, Royal Roads University, University of Victoria, BC Transit, British Columbia Housing Management Commission, Health, Indigenous Relations and Reconciliation, Parliamentary Secretary for Gender Equity, Member(s) of the BC Legislative Assembly, Tourism, Arts, Culture and Sport, Education and Child Care, Emergency Management and C